  <joint name="casterwheel_joint" type="fixed">
    <!--
      Ground contact heights, all measured from the mesh files rather than
      assumed:

        leftwheel.STL / rightwheel.STL  radius 0.08294  (bbox 0.16588 across)
        wheel contact plane            -0.063 - 0.08294 = -0.14594
        casterwheel.STL                reaches 0.03791 below its link origin

      so a caster joint sits at -0.14594 + 0.03791 = -0.10803 for its underside
      to meet the same ground plane as the wheels.

      This was briefly -0.0948, computed from an assumed caster radius of
      0.0512 instead of the mesh's actual 0.03791 drop. That lifted both
      casters 13.2 mm clear of the floor, so the robot balanced on the two
      drive wheels alone and tipped nose-down 2 deg onto the front caster,
      dragging it as a skid: it reached only 14% of commanded speed
      (0.021 m/s against 0.15 m/s commanded) and nav2 aborted continually.
    -->
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.25029 -0.01098 -0.10803"/>
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="casterwheel_link"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <!--
    ROMR has one caster, at the FRONT (x=+0.250). A second caster was added at
    x=-0.20 to extend the support polygon rearward, because the robot tipped
    backwards when it struck a door frame at 0.35 m/s. It has been removed
    again: two wheels plus two casters is four contact points on a rigid body,
    which is statically over-constrained, and the solver's continuous attempt to
    satisfy all four showed up as the robot creeping while parked.

    Three contacts are determinate. The tipping it was added to prevent was
    itself compounded by two defects since fixed — caster height taken from an
    assumed radius rather than the mesh, and an acceleration limit eighteen
    times below what the controller assumed — so it is no longer clear the
    fourth contact was ever the right remedy. Rear tipping margin returns to
    24.1 deg; if tipping recurs, widen the wheelbase or move mass forward rather
    than adding a fourth ground contact.
  -->
  <link name="lidar_link">
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.00050486 -3.7855E-05 -0.025753"/>
      <mass value="0.2064"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.00011156" ixy="-1.2701E-07" ixz="-1.6019E-06" iyy="0.00010988" iyz="1.201E-07" izz="0.0001391"/>
